-5d8b-b29a-5733b229fb9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066106 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066106 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066106 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066106 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066106 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3c1bfb5b-8661-579d-bbb2-a888c6e3cec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066130 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066130 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066130 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066130 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066130 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "87a569e7-b9ff-5249-82a9-9efcf843d4cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066131 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066131 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066131 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066131 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066131 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3e1b4ecb-e59f-573e-9fd5-712b1fdac694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068481 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068481 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1$ sketched below.\n\n![](images/e6/e63b2ac5fe2bdfb905fe3c86706be4a556e5322698f9fd26ee115dba0a886f4b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5cf6d36a-0e67-5f16-befd-32d30cf4b930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068481 #edb/ref 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+3 \\\\ y=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+3$ into the second equation $y=x-1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =x-1 \\\\ 5x & =x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1, \\, y=-2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068482 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068482 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the lines $x + 2 y = 1$ and $2 x - y = 7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fe8c6a0-f152-5349-9152-b4c018368dbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068482 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=1 \\\\ 2x-y=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ term in the first equation:\n\n![](images/d2/d2cac87bca62cf4fa8cc6ad3c0bd289de7cb0f8c39e742dd6e7731cb90b0e354.svg)\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{2y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ (x+4x) & =(1+14) \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=3$ into the equation $x+2y=1$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =1 \\\\ 3+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-2 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=3, \\, y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(3,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068483 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068483 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = -3 x + a$ and $y = x - 1$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b, -4).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cceef15c-a242-5486-a830-f6bdf9f07010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068483 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(b,-4)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b, \\, y=-4$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+a \\\\ y=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b, \\, y=-4$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4=-3b+a \\\\ -4=b-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the second equation, we can solve for $b\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =b-1 \\\\ -3 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $a\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-3b+a \\\\ -4 & =-3(-3)+a \\\\ -4 & =9+a \\\\ -13 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -13$ and $b = -3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068713 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e122a1fe-f340-5af6-9908-68ad52f89731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068714 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4a81c1cd-7381-5788-8a51-53cecee5f600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068715 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Unknown Parameters Given a Point of Intersection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068715 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068715 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b154eb5d-a45b-5d92-9e33-dd7875327e6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068945 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "694f32b9-ef02-56c2-a6ab-8b0b5d3806a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069112 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069112 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069112 #edb/ref 1069 "q-208830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069112 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069112 #edb/ref 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069112 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5474d40-cfd8-5d52-b2b0-e62e8f7d76fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069112 #edb/ref 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-1 \\\\ y=-4x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=3x-1$ into the second equation $y=-4x+6$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =-4x+6 \\\\ 3x & =-4x+7 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,1,2\\,).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069128 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069128 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069128 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069128 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069128 #edb/ref 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=7$ and $3x+2y=11$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069128 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce310a81-63ea-5437-b6a7-508ae82b0078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069128 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ 3x+2y=11 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=14 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{2y} & =11 \\\\ (2x+3x) & =(14+11) \\\\ 5x & =25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=5$ into the equation $x-y=7$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =7 \\\\ 5-y & =7 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=5,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(5,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069598 #edb/ref 1061 143915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ca9afc5-3404-515f-b1c6-ea4d5161c29a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069599 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069599 #edb/ref 1061 143916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069599 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "787639d6-4b54-5b06-ab03-6d845df01b6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069599 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069600 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069600 #edb/ref 1061 143920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069600 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69dbdcc7-0c22-5579-903a-39ea4acc15fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069600 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069601 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069601 #edb/ref 1061 143921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5fda47d7-4b73-57eb-a3db-30d707f29f26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069602 #edb/ref 1061 143925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b997cef-985b-5abc-bc48-d1fbfa6ebd29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070021 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the point of intersection between the two lines $y = x + 2$ and $y = 2x + 1.$ A sketch of both lines and the point of intersection $P$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bf429fdfbe706521b81617f56a0f561171aa7fd48e0af89318225f1d328e667.png)\n\nThe point $P$ consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=x+2$ and $y=2x+1.$ So, to find the coordinates of $P$ we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=x+2$ into the second equation $y=2x+1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =2x \\\\ 1 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1, \\, y=3.$ So, the point of intersection has the coordinates $(1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070021 #edb/ref 1065 8709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070021 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "39070934-9f3c-5858-8959-365242cf0563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070021 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070034 #edb/ref 1064 "When two lines are given in the *standard form*, you may find it easier to use the *elimination method* to find the point of intersection.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070034 #edb/ref 1065 9140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070034 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "efdf9704-19aa-5a4a-8ac3-220f267bc227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070034 #edb/ref 1067 "Lines Given in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530869 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530869 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04a54877-c197-56e8-954f-97fa34f640d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530885 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530885 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530885 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b8feaa1-6c6b-5ce2-95a0-abd65447e29e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530895 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530895 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530895 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "116be24f-5fd6-5889-ac0f-f72b509821b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530917 #edb/ref 1080 "(2,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530917 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530917 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b42dbf5-902f-50e2-aab3-1a9d25bf1aa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530926 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f9a098d-ebc1-504f-8f6e-6368dd1908a1" #edb/ref 1759218604541