5b95-b337-f167d5c09c50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531558 #edb/ref 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=6$  and $2x+3y=2$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e71161b-a139-5715-8ffb-5d9680416a1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531558 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=6 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $3,$ then we will get a $-3y$ term that can cancel with the $3y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=6 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3y=18 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ 2x+\\cancel{3y} & =2 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(18+2) \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=4$ back into the equation $x-y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =6 \\\\ 4-y & =6 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=4,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(4,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531564 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $4x+y=a$ and $7x+2y=-2$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(-2,b).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1afc104b-55b0-5a4c-8495-1467f6f4a4e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531564 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(-2,b)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=-2,\\,y=b$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y=a \\\\ 7x+2y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=-2,\\,y=b$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+b=a \\\\ 7(-2)+2b=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8+b=a \\\\ b=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we know that $b=6.$ Then, we can substitute $b=6$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8+b & =a \\\\ -8+6 & =a \\\\ -2 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531570 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531570 #edb/ref 1069 "q-12345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531570 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531570 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2377eeaf-5209-5b0e-a180-97a6667f01fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531570 #edb/ref 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=4x+3 \\\\ y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=4x+3$ into the second equation $y=-x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & =-x-2 \\\\ 5x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=-x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x-2 \\\\  & =-(-1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1$ and $y=-1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531585 #edb/ref 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $4x+3y=5$ and $-x-6y=4$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f0c0760-2f71-5da7-a09a-417c59aab4e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531585 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=5 \\\\ -x-6y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $6y$ term that can cancel with the $-6y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=5 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ -x-6y=4 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x+6y=10 \\\\ -x-6y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+\\cancel{6y} & =10 \\\\ -x-\\cancel{6y} & =4 \\\\ (8x-x) & =(10+4) \\\\ 7x & =14 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=2$ into the equation $4x+3y=5$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y & =5 \\\\ 4(2)+3y & =5 \\\\ 8+3y & =5 \\\\ 3y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=2,\\,y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(2,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531607 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531607 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531607 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531607 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531607 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $y=-2x+2$ and $y=-5x-a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b,8).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531607 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f0810d4-95d5-5ec1-a80a-fff5b7ccd8fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531607 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(b,8)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b,\\,y=8$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+2 \\\\ y=-5x-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b,\\,y=8$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=-2(b)+2 \\\\ 8=-5(b)-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=-2b+2 \\\\ 8=-5b-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $b=-3.$ Then, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =-5b-a \\\\ 8 & =-5(-3)-a \\\\ 8 & =15-a \\\\ -7 & =-a \\\\ 7 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7$ and $b=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531619 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531619 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531619 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531619 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531619 #edb/ref 1071 "The point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531619 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fea67f7-ec4c-5ab9-8c67-4d43507c8b87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531619 #edb/ref 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+2 \\\\ y=-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+2$ into the second equation $y=-2x-5$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & =-2x-5 \\\\ 5x & =-2x-7 \\\\ 7x & =-7 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=5x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x+2 \\\\  & =5(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-5+2 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1,\\,y=-3.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,-1,-3\\,).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531659 #edb/ref 1069 "q-12341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531659 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531659 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbb8ab84-c16d-554a-bf88-2b57aaa4afbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531659 #edb/ref 1074 "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+4 \\\\ y=3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=-3x+4$ into the second equation $y=3x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4 & =3x-2 \\\\ -6x & =-6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(1)-2 \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are  $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
